<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    ServiceNow Related Lists record-definition for the DEFAULT VIEW of the
    `x_casemgmt_case` table in the scoped application `x_casemgmt`.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
      AAP Section 0.4.4 requires the build to configure "Related Lists on the
      case form for both `case_task` (parent = current case) and `case_party`
      (parent = current case)". That is the mechanism by which an internal
      user drives a case end to end from one screen: the case form has to
      show the case's own tasks (so the operator can see what still blocks a
      Resolved transition) and the case's parties (so the operator can see
      who the case is about).

      Until this file was added the package carried NO related-list record at
      all, and none existed on the verification instance either: a query of
      `sys_ui_related_list` for `nameSTARTSWITHx_casemgmt` returned zero rows.
      The consequence was measured on the running application: the case form
      rendered its `#related_lists_wrapper` container with a height of zero,
      the form's "Configure Related Lists" slushbucket had an empty Selected
      column, and the child rows that DO exist were unreachable from the
      parent record. On CASE0000981 those unreachable children were
      TASK0000276 (Open), TASK0000277 (Closed), PARTY0000159 (Person) and
      PARTY0000160 (Organization) - i.e. the data was correct all along and
      only the presentation metadata was missing.

      Nothing else was wrong. The child tables already carry the reference
      field the related list keys on (`x_casemgmt_case_task.case` and
      `x_casemgmt_case_party.case`, both mandatory references to
      `x_casemgmt_case` per AAP Section 0.5.7), and the read ACLs already
      permit the parent's audience to read those children. The only missing
      artifact was the one below.

    WHAT THIS FILE PRODUCES ON THE CASE FORM
        position 0  ->  Case Tasks    (x_casemgmt_case_task.case)
        position 1  ->  Case Parties  (x_casemgmt_case_party.case)
      Tasks come first because the task list is what gates the case
      lifecycle: AAP Section 0.5.5 blocks the In Progress to Resolved
      transition until every linked task has status Closed, with the verbatim
      error "All tasks must be closed before resolving this case." An
      operator who cannot see the task list cannot see why the transition was
      refused, so the task list is the first thing the form should show below
      the fields. Parties follow, matching the order the AAP itself uses when
      it names the two lists.

      No third related list is declared. AAP Section 0.7.2's MINIMAL CHANGE
      CLAUSE means only the two lists the AAP names are configured, and the
      sibling tables get no related-list record of their own because the AAP
      asks for related lists on the case form only.

    THE `related_list` VALUE FORMAT (measured, not assumed)
      Each entry's `related_list` column is the string `<child table>.<field
      on the child that references this table>`. This was verified against the
      out-of-box related lists for the `incident` table on the verification
      instance, whose seven entries read exactly:
          task_sla.task, task_ci.task, task_cmdb_ci_service.task,
          task_service_offering.task, incident.parent_incident,
          sc_request.parent, interaction_related_record.task
      So `x_casemgmt_case_task.case` means "every Case Task whose `case`
      field points at the record on screen". A relationship-backed list would
      instead read `REL:<sys_id of the sys_relationship>`; no relationship
      record is needed here, and none is created, because both child tables
      reach the parent through an ordinary reference field.

    SERIALIZATION SHAPE (why the two entries live inside this one file)
      `sys_ui_related_list` extends Application File (`sys_metadata`), so it
      has `sys_scope`, `sys_package` and `sys_update_name` and can be carried
      as an Update Set record in its own right.

      `sys_ui_related_list_entry` does NOT extend Application File - its
      `super_class` is empty and it has no `sys_scope`, no `sys_package` and
      no `sys_update_name`. An entry therefore cannot be keyed as an Update
      Set record of its own, which is why both entries are serialized inside
      the same payload as their parent rather than as separate records. This
      mirrors `sys_ui_list_element` under
      ../list_layouts/sys_ui_list_x_casemgmt_case_default.xml, which is the
      same structural situation on the sibling table.

      The records below are emitted as sibling elements of one `<unload>`,
      which is this package's dominant payload convention (782 of the 925
      records in ../update-set/ use an `<unload>` root) and is the shape the
      platform itself produces when it exports records. Each element carries
      action="INSERT_OR_UPDATE" so a re-import updates the existing rows
      instead of duplicating them.

    THE `view` FIELD HOLDS THE LITERAL STRING "Default view"
      `sys_ui_related_list.view` is declared as a reference to `sys_ui_view`,
      yet every out-of-box row for a default-view related list stores the
      literal text "Default view" in it - verified on the verification
      instance against `sys_ui_related_incident_null`,
      `sys_ui_related_sys_ux_children_null` and
      `sys_ui_related_sys_rollback_sequence_null`, none of which point at a
      real `sys_ui_view` row. A NAMED view instead stores that view's sys_id
      (for example `sys_ui_related_cmdb_ci_hpux_server_cmdb_browse`). The
      same asymmetry is documented on the sibling list-layout artifact. This
      record is the DEFAULT view, so it stores the literal string, and the
      `<view/>` attribute-free form is deliberately not used.

    THE `sys_user` FIELD MUST STAY EMPTY
      `sys_ui_related_list.sys_user` distinguishes a BASE definition (empty -
      applies to everyone) from a PERSONALIZED one (a single user's own
      choice of related lists, which silently overrides the base definition
      for that user only). This record is the BASE definition, so
      `<sys_user/>` is empty and every user of the application gets these two
      lists. A verifier who finds a personalized row for this table should
      delete it, because while one exists that user does not see the shipped
      configuration. Personalized rows are per-user state, are never carried
      by an Update Set, and are not part of this application.

    NO DATA IS AFFECTED, AND NO SECURITY IS GRANTED
      A related list only decides which child lists a form renders. It
      creates no records, changes no field values, and grants nothing: what
      each role can actually see in these lists is still decided by the
      table-level and field-level ACLs in ../acl/. A `x_casemgmt_case_agent`
      viewing an assigned case sees that case's tasks and parties; a
      `x_casemgmt_case_viewer` sees them read only; neither gains access to
      any row the ACL matrix in ../docs/acl-matrix.md does not already allow.

    A CACHE CAVEAT THAT WAS MEASURED, NOT GUESSED
      Creating these three rows is necessary but, on an instance that has
      already rendered the case form once, not sufficient. The platform caches
      the related-list definition for a (table, view) pair server side. If the
      form was rendered BEFORE the definition existed, the server has cached
      the empty answer, and inserting the rows afterwards through a non-UI
      path (a direct REST insert, or a record import) does not invalidate that
      cache. The observable symptom is precise and misleading: the
      "Configure > Related Lists" slushbucket correctly shows both entries in
      its Selected column, and the rows read back correctly from the tables,
      yet the form still renders `#related_lists_wrapper` at zero height with
      no list markup in the document at all and no related-list request on the
      wire. A control table on the same instance in the same session renders
      its own related lists normally, so nothing is broken except the cached
      answer for this one table.

      This was reproduced and then resolved on the verification instance. What
      does NOT clear it: re-writing the same values over REST (an update that
      dirties no field is a no-op, so no business rule fires). What DOES clear
      it: re-saving the definition through the platform's own UI - open
      "Configure > Related Lists" on the case form and press Save with the two
      entries already selected. That processor deletes and reinserts the
      definition through the path that invalidates the cache, after which the
      form renders both lists immediately and keeps rendering them across
      fresh loads. Flushing the instance cache is the heavier alternative.

      A normal Update Set commit invalidates metadata caches as part of
      committing, so a clean install is not expected to need this. It is
      documented here because it is the one failure mode of this artifact that
      looks like a bad record definition and is not, and because a verifier
      who applies these rows by hand rather than by commit WILL hit it. The
      same note appears in ../docs/deployment.md.

    IDENTIFIERS
      The three sys_ids below are pinned to the rows that the platform's own
      related-list processor created on the verification instance, so this
      file describes exactly the configuration that was observed rendering
      both lists correctly. This is the same provenance rule the sibling
      artifact ../list_layouts/sys_ui_list_x_casemgmt_case_default.xml
      follows: prefer the identifiers the platform itself minted for this
      table over locally invented ones.
        a799833a93a6cf10830ef82bdd03d6ad   the definition
        6799833a93a6cf10830ef82bdd03d6ae   entry, position 0, Case Tasks
        eb99833a93a6cf10830ef82bdd03d6ae   entry, position 1, Case Parties
      An earlier revision of this file carried locally derived MD5 identifiers
      instead. They were replaced when the definition was re-saved through the
      platform UI to clear the cache described above, because that processor
      deletes and reinserts its rows rather than updating them in place, and
      leaving the old values here would have meant shipping identifiers that
      exist on no instance.

      The identifiers are not load-bearing. The import engine matches the
      definition on `sys_update_name`
      (`sys_ui_related_x_casemgmt_case_null`, the platform's own naming for a
      related-list definition on a null/default view), so a target instance
      that already holds a definition for this table updates it rather than
      gaining a second one. No reference field in this file points at a
      foreign record by sys_id: the only sys_id-valued reference is each
      entry's `list_id`, and it points at the definition declared in this same
      document.

    CROSS-REFERENCES (AAP Section 0.5.2 dependency ordering)
      MUST LOAD AFTER:
        - ../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml (scope authority)
        - ../tables/x_casemgmt_case.xml (the table whose form is configured)
        - ../tables/x_casemgmt_case_task.xml and
          ../tables/x_casemgmt_case_party.xml (the child tables named in the
          two entries; a related list whose child table does not yet exist is
          dropped on import)
        - ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_task_case.xml and
          ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_party_case.xml (the reference fields
          the two entries key on)
      MUST LOAD BEFORE:
        - ../seed-data/tasks/x_casemgmt_case_task_demo_*.xml and
          ../seed-data/parties/x_casemgmt_case_party_demo_*.xml (so the demo
          children are visible from their parent case immediately after
          commit, which is what AAP Section 0.7.1 asks a verifier to confirm)
      In ../update-set/x_casemgmt_case_management_update_set.xml this record
      is placed immediately after the List Layout record for the same table,
      because both are Default-view presentation metadata for
      `x_casemgmt_case` and both have the same predecessors.

    AAP COMPLIANCE
      - AAP 0.4.4: related lists configured on the case form for case_task
        and case_party, in that order.
      - AAP 0.3.1: a serialized record-definition under
        servicenow-case-management-poc/, covered by the section's
        `**/*.xml` wildcard.
      - AAP 0.7.2 scoped-namespace exclusivity: the definition carries the
        scoped application's sys_scope and sys_package; no global related
        list is touched, and no out-of-box table's form is modified.
      - AAP 0.7.2 minimal change: exactly the two lists the AAP names, on
        exactly the one form the AAP names.
      - AAP 0.7.2 no hard-coded sys_id in a cross-artifact reference: the
        only reference is internal to this document.
      - No PII: table and field names only.
-->
<unload unload_date="2025-01-01 00:00:00">
    <!--
        ===================================================================
        RECORD [1] OF 3 - RELATED LIST DEFINITION (sys_ui_related_list)
        ===================================================================
        The Default-view related-list definition for the case table. `name`
        holds the table whose form is being configured; `calculated_name` is
        the platform's display value for the row and follows the out-of-box
        pattern "<Table label> - <view>" (measured on
        sys_ui_related_incident_null, whose calculated_name is
        "Incident - Default view").

        The definition's own `related_list`, `position`, `order_by` and
        `filter` columns stay empty. They exist on this table but are unused
        by the current platform serialization: the per-list values live on
        the entry rows below. Every out-of-box definition row on the
        verification instance leaves them empty, and a query for definitions
        with a populated `related_list` returned zero rows instance-wide.
    -->
    <sys_ui_related_list action="INSERT_OR_UPDATE">
        <calculated_name>Case - Default view</calculated_name>
        <filter/>
        <name>x_casemgmt_case</name>
        <order_by/>
        <position/>
        <related_list/>
        <sys_class_name>sys_ui_related_list</sys_class_name>
        <sys_created_by>admin</sys_created_by>
        <sys_created_on>2025-01-01 00:00:00</sys_created_on>
        <sys_domain>global</sys_domain>
        <sys_domain_path>/</sys_domain_path>
        <sys_id>a799833a93a6cf10830ef82bdd03d6ad</sys_id>
        <sys_mod_count>0</sys_mod_count>
        <sys_name>Case - Default view</sys_name>
        <sys_package display_value="x_casemgmt Case Management" source="x_casemgmt">82b99028936f74320d74d6f88357a5af</sys_package>
        <sys_policy/>
        <sys_scope display_value="x_casemgmt Case Management">82b99028936f74320d74d6f88357a5af</sys_scope>
        <sys_update_name>sys_ui_related_x_casemgmt_case_null</sys_update_name>
        <sys_updated_by>admin</sys_updated_by>
        <sys_updated_on>2025-01-01 00:00:00</sys_updated_on>
        <sys_user/>
        <view>Default view</view>
        <view_name/>
    </sys_ui_related_list>
    <!--
        ===================================================================
        RECORD [2] OF 3 - CASE TASKS ENTRY (sys_ui_related_list_entry)
        ===================================================================
        Renders every Case Task whose `case` reference points at the record
        on screen. `filter` is empty on purpose: the list must show ALL of
        the case's tasks, including the Closed ones, because the operator's
        question at the Resolved gate is "which tasks are not Closed yet",
        and that question cannot be answered by a list that hides the
        Closed ones. `order_by` is empty so the list uses the child table's
        own default order, and `group_by_field` is empty so the list renders
        flat rather than grouped.
    -->
    <sys_ui_related_list_entry action="INSERT_OR_UPDATE">
        <filter/>
        <group_by_field/>
        <list_id display_value="Case - Default view">a799833a93a6cf10830ef82bdd03d6ad</list_id>
        <order_by/>
        <position>0</position>
        <related_list>x_casemgmt_case_task.case</related_list>
        <sys_class_name>sys_ui_related_list_entry</sys_class_name>
        <sys_created_by>admin</sys_created_by>
        <sys_created_on>2025-01-01 00:00:00</sys_created_on>
        <sys_id>6799833a93a6cf10830ef82bdd03d6ae</sys_id>
        <sys_mod_count>0</sys_mod_count>
        <sys_updated_by>admin</sys_updated_by>
        <sys_updated_on>2025-01-01 00:00:00</sys_updated_on>
    </sys_ui_related_list_entry>
    <!--
        ===================================================================
        RECORD [3] OF 3 - CASE PARTIES ENTRY (sys_ui_related_list_entry)
        ===================================================================
        Renders every Case Party whose `case` reference points at the record
        on screen, so the polymorphic party rows (party_type Person, with a
        `person` reference to sys_user; or party_type Organization, with an
        `organization` reference to core_company) are reachable from the
        case they belong to. AAP Section 0.7.1 lists "People and
        Organizations associated to cases as typed parties" among the
        success criteria, and this entry is what makes that association
        visible from the case.
    -->
    <sys_ui_related_list_entry action="INSERT_OR_UPDATE">
        <filter/>
        <group_by_field/>
        <list_id display_value="Case - Default view">a799833a93a6cf10830ef82bdd03d6ad</list_id>
        <order_by/>
        <position>1</position>
        <related_list>x_casemgmt_case_party.case</related_list>
        <sys_class_name>sys_ui_related_list_entry</sys_class_name>
        <sys_created_by>admin</sys_created_by>
        <sys_created_on>2025-01-01 00:00:00</sys_created_on>
        <sys_id>eb99833a93a6cf10830ef82bdd03d6ae</sys_id>
        <sys_mod_count>0</sys_mod_count>
        <sys_updated_by>admin</sys_updated_by>
        <sys_updated_on>2025-01-01 00:00:00</sys_updated_on>
    </sys_ui_related_list_entry>
</unload>
